# Hero section layout rule

Every two-column hero (left = copy/CTA, right = visual/demo), on **640px+
viewports only**:

- Columns stretch to equal height *to each other* (grid/flex stretch on both
  sides, matching whichever column's natural content is taller — not
  shrink-to-content on one while the other stretches).
- Don't stretch the column-pair row itself to fill the whole fixed hero
  height (`flex: 0 1 auto` on the row, sized to content — not `1 1 auto`).
  Center that naturally-sized row inside the hero (`justify-content: center`
  on the hero) so leftover space becomes one balanced margin above/below the
  whole content block, not padding stretched inside each column.
- Within a column shorter than the row (e.g. copy vs. a taller visual
  widget), cap its internal gap (e.g. `gap: clamp(28px, 4vh, 56px)`) and
  center it (`justify-content: center`) — never `margin-top: auto` pushing a
  footer to the very bottom of a column (that leaves one oversized dead gap
  instead of even spacing).
- Hero height = `100svh - navbar height - announcement-strip height`, via
  `--home-hero-h: calc(100svh - var(--home-nav-h) - var(--home-strip-h))`
  (`svh` stays stable while mobile URL chrome shows/hides; avoid `vh`/`dvh`).
  `--home-nav-h` must switch value at the same breakpoints as `Navbar`'s own
  height (54px / 57px / 77px at <640 / 640–1023 / 1024+); drive the sticky
  header border-box from that var.
- Add `max-height` tiers so short-but-wide viewports (landscape phones) fall
  back to `height: auto` instead of clipping content.

**Below 640px:** hero stacks in one column, natural height — none of the
above applies.

Full rationale, exact px values, and known limitations live in `AGENTS.md`
under "Hero section layout rule (sm and wider)" — that is the canonical
source. If the rule changes, update AGENTS.md first and mirror the bullets
above.
